1
00:00:02,012 --> 00:00:05,053
[SPEAKER_01]: I was thinking about the Metta Sutta.

2
00:00:08,935 --> 00:00:12,156
[SPEAKER_01]: And just going through that, there's quite a lot in it, actually.

3
00:00:13,797 --> 00:00:17,039
[SPEAKER_01]: So we can stop wherever feels right.

4
00:00:20,920 --> 00:00:22,701
[SPEAKER_01]: So I'll read it verse by verse.

5
00:00:26,956 --> 00:00:34,380
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it begins with, what should be done by one's skillful in good, so as to gain the state of peace?

6
00:00:39,042 --> 00:00:40,163
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of a question, really.

7
00:00:42,584 --> 00:00:44,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, what we spoke before.

8
00:00:47,607 --> 00:00:52,289
[SPEAKER_00]: Only the person with the right view knows good as good and bad as bad.

9
00:00:54,597 --> 00:00:57,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Knows kusala is kusala and akusala is akusala.

10
00:00:59,901 --> 00:01:03,605
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, that also it applies to somebody in the right view.

11
00:01:06,027 --> 00:01:11,511
[SPEAKER_00]: What should be done once you know kusala is kusala and akusala is akusala?

12
00:01:11,871 --> 00:01:13,153
[SPEAKER_01]: One who is skillful in good.

13
00:01:13,173 --> 00:01:13,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

14
00:01:14,253 --> 00:01:16,135
[SPEAKER_00]: One who knows good is good, bad is bad.

15
00:01:16,595 --> 00:01:20,118
[SPEAKER_01]: What should he do to have his mind in a peaceful state?

16
00:01:23,413 --> 00:01:26,915
[SPEAKER_01]: It says, let him be able, upright and straightforward.

17
00:01:29,937 --> 00:01:32,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Isn't he already like that?

18
00:01:32,558 --> 00:01:34,239
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, there's always room for improvement.

19
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:40,363
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the paññā is upright and straightforward from Puthujjana's point of view, but not from an arahant's point of view.

20
00:01:40,623 --> 00:01:48,968
[SPEAKER_01]: It says, it is easy to speak to, gentle and not proud.

21
00:01:51,601 --> 00:01:54,443
[SPEAKER_01]: And this gentle got me.

22
00:01:54,803 --> 00:01:57,185
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not... he's not sensitive.

23
00:01:58,346 --> 00:01:58,566
No.

24
00:02:00,187 --> 00:02:00,527
[SPEAKER_01]: You know.

25
00:02:01,368 --> 00:02:02,448
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's not what gentle is.

26
00:02:03,649 --> 00:02:06,791
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you're non-gentle when you have intentions of harm.

27
00:02:08,092 --> 00:02:13,796
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're unable to have... to act out of intentions of harm, you're gentle.

28
00:02:14,777 --> 00:02:19,140
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean you will necessarily come across as kind and gentle.

29
00:02:20,260 --> 00:02:20,581
[SPEAKER_00]: Because

30
00:02:21,750 --> 00:02:24,171
[SPEAKER_00]: People don't have criteria for what truly kind and gentle is.

31
00:02:27,373 --> 00:02:27,933
[SPEAKER_00]: Or proud.

32
00:02:28,853 --> 00:02:29,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

33
00:02:29,754 --> 00:02:30,414
[SPEAKER_00]: Not proud.

34
00:02:30,474 --> 00:02:32,695
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot be rooted in your vanity, in your conceit.

35
00:02:35,577 --> 00:02:37,838
[SPEAKER_01]: He's contented.

36
00:02:38,778 --> 00:02:40,319
[SPEAKER_01]: Easily, easy to support.

37
00:02:41,139 --> 00:02:42,460
[SPEAKER_00]: Easily satisfied, yeah.

38
00:02:43,580 --> 00:02:46,522
[SPEAKER_01]: With few tasks and living very lightly.

39
00:02:48,177 --> 00:02:53,281
[SPEAKER_01]: These faculties are serene, sure, are serene, prudent and modest.

40
00:02:53,681 --> 00:02:55,863
[SPEAKER_01]: This is interesting.

41
00:02:56,183 --> 00:02:58,785
[SPEAKER_01]: Unswayed by the emotions of the clans.

42
00:02:59,866 --> 00:03:04,209
[SPEAKER_01]: So, unswayed by the emotions of others.

43
00:03:04,529 --> 00:03:05,750
[SPEAKER_00]: Of others, yeah.

44
00:03:05,770 --> 00:03:06,671
[SPEAKER_00]: Of other authorities.

45
00:03:07,571 --> 00:03:09,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Of other traditions, of other establishments.

46
00:03:11,394 --> 00:03:16,618
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, when you say, oh, that the rules of a clan means, if you belong to it, you must obey by that.

47
00:03:17,607 --> 00:03:25,795
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like belonging to a greater, eh... identity, which is usually everybody does... belongs to some greater identity to some extent.

48
00:03:26,936 --> 00:03:32,141
[SPEAKER_00]: Be it culture, religion, education or something makes you belong to the... to that group identity.

49
00:03:33,963 --> 00:03:36,185
[SPEAKER_00]: But that actually requires a wrong view.

50
00:03:38,060 --> 00:03:40,163
[SPEAKER_00]: It's rooted in the wrong view, that kind of belonging.

51
00:03:40,503 --> 00:03:43,586
[SPEAKER_00]: It's rooted in you not being transparent in regard to your own individuality.

52
00:03:44,107 --> 00:03:56,299
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the one who has the right view, who has developed his mind, he cannot, then, eh... ignore the fact that he always remains enclosed within himself.

53
00:03:58,984 --> 00:04:07,651
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, if he chooses to follow what clan says you should do, he'll only do so because he knows good is good and bad is bad, not because the clan said so.

54
00:04:08,011 --> 00:04:14,817
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't know for yourself, you will be going by the words of others, by the authority of others, by the majority of others.

55
00:04:14,857 --> 00:04:15,197
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

56
00:04:15,217 --> 00:04:22,463
[SPEAKER_00]: That's another way of... Oh, well, look, I don't understand this, I don't understand that, but this is more numerous.

57
00:04:22,503 --> 00:04:25,165
[SPEAKER_00]: There's more people saying this than that.

58
00:04:25,205 --> 00:04:27,527
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'll just go with the majority on this one.

59
00:04:28,518 --> 00:04:30,179
[SPEAKER_00]: Means, either way, you don't understand for yourself.

60
00:04:30,199 --> 00:04:34,220
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even, say, if that majority were right, you are in the wrong.

61
00:04:34,780 --> 00:04:38,001
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're not understanding for yourself, you're going just by the majority of it.

62
00:04:38,061 --> 00:04:38,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

63
00:04:38,622 --> 00:04:43,283
[SPEAKER_01]: You're being swayed by... you've got no grounding yourself.

64
00:04:43,303 --> 00:04:44,044
[SPEAKER_01]: No grounding, yeah.

65
00:04:44,124 --> 00:04:46,004
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just circumstantial.

66
00:04:46,344 --> 00:04:55,928
[SPEAKER_00]: Circumstantially occurred that, in this instance, majority was right and... but you, as a rule of thumb, go with the majority out of your own weakness and, basically, lack of understanding.

67
00:04:56,608 --> 00:05:13,679
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, literally, like for Sotāpanna, when he develops the right view, when he knows kusala is kusala, kusala is kusala, is the sutta, say, even if the whole world would say the Buddha was wrong or that's wrong, if he knows that as right, he would go with what he knows is right.

68
00:05:14,279 --> 00:05:18,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, from Puthujjana's point of view, that sounds like an extremely arrogant and proud position.

69
00:05:19,262 --> 00:05:20,383
[SPEAKER_00]: Who do you think you are?

70
00:05:20,443 --> 00:05:22,444
[SPEAKER_00]: You know better than the entire world.

71
00:05:24,543 --> 00:05:27,304
[SPEAKER_00]: But, it's factually, he knows better than the entire world.

72
00:05:27,644 --> 00:05:28,564
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Sotāpanna is.

73
00:05:29,365 --> 00:05:32,866
[SPEAKER_01]: But he doesn't just think he knows better than the entire world in every single case.

74
00:05:32,986 --> 00:05:34,026
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

75
00:05:34,186 --> 00:05:38,868
[SPEAKER_00]: His thoughts are not rooted in I am better than the entire world, I am worse, or I am equal.

76
00:05:39,448 --> 00:05:45,490
[SPEAKER_00]: His thoughts are rooted in recognizing kusala is a kusala, kusala is kusala, a kusala is a kusala.

77
00:05:46,484 --> 00:05:48,986
[SPEAKER_00]: So, wholesome is wholesome, unwholesome is unwholesome.

78
00:05:49,607 --> 00:05:55,852
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if the whole world says this is unwholesome, but he knows that to be wholesome, he will not go with the entire world.

79
00:05:58,334 --> 00:06:05,801
[SPEAKER_00]: If the whole world says, yes, this is wholesome, and he knows that it's wholesome, he'll go with it, because it's wholesome, not because the entire world said so.

80
00:06:07,182 --> 00:06:12,427
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how he will not be swayed by what clans say to do, because he has the internal criteria.

81
00:06:13,780 --> 00:06:34,511
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will never develop that internal criteria if you never basically look beyond and make effort to understand things for yourself on that individual basis, but instead always seek shelter in the safety of numbers, crowd, traditions, authority, which is the whole Kalama Sutta.

82
00:06:34,831 --> 00:06:39,514
[SPEAKER_00]: Do not go by the majority for its own sake, by the tradition.

83
00:06:40,474 --> 00:06:41,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Find out for yourself.

84
00:06:43,012 --> 00:06:44,853
[SPEAKER_00]: And many people kind of think they understand that.

85
00:06:44,913 --> 00:06:47,813
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yes, of course, find out for myself, experience for myself.

86
00:06:47,893 --> 00:06:48,554
[SPEAKER_00]: Personal truth.

87
00:06:48,614 --> 00:06:53,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, when it comes to drilling their views down, it's going to be, well, most people say this.

88
00:06:54,095 --> 00:06:57,936
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I'm right, because I just subscribe to whatever else people say.

89
00:06:59,077 --> 00:07:01,177
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but do you understand that as right?

90
00:07:01,777 --> 00:07:02,418
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's right?

91
00:07:03,398 --> 00:07:06,819
[SPEAKER_00]: Or do you just basically seek the safety in the group?

92
00:07:10,896 --> 00:07:13,457
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you do, you'll never develop the right view.

93
00:07:15,337 --> 00:07:17,238
[SPEAKER_01]: Or have a peaceful mind.

94
00:07:17,258 --> 00:07:19,399
[SPEAKER_00]: You will not have a peaceful mind without the right view.

95
00:07:20,759 --> 00:07:22,400
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no peace without understanding.

96
00:07:22,900 --> 00:07:24,480
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no understanding without the right view.

97
00:07:27,521 --> 00:07:29,222
[SPEAKER_00]: There is safety in the numbers, by the way.

98
00:07:29,702 --> 00:07:34,663
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that the safety you get from the group or majority or belonging to a tradition or a group identity.

99
00:07:35,544 --> 00:07:37,044
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that it's not safe.

100
00:07:37,264 --> 00:07:38,144
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that it's not real.

101
00:07:38,465 --> 00:07:38,765
[SPEAKER_00]: It is.

102
00:07:39,065 --> 00:07:39,725
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's flawed.

103
00:07:41,380 --> 00:07:42,521
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not a true safety.

104
00:07:45,545 --> 00:07:47,447
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not the safety that you seek.

105
00:07:49,330 --> 00:07:53,134
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's only something that's sort of closest to safety that you know.

106
00:07:53,355 --> 00:07:57,540
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you look at it properly, investigate it, you'll realize actually it's not the safety.

107
00:07:59,162 --> 00:08:00,043
[SPEAKER_00]: It's safety of ignorance.

108
00:08:01,120 --> 00:08:03,601
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter what the size of the group is.

109
00:08:04,121 --> 00:08:07,642
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact is, I don't know for myself that what they say is right.

110
00:08:08,082 --> 00:08:13,684
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, it's on the level of my choosing to believe that they are right, because it makes me feel comfortable.

111
00:08:15,465 --> 00:08:17,226
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if they happen to be, why...

112
00:08:17,286 --> 00:08:18,086
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all secondary.

113
00:08:18,166 --> 00:08:19,006
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all secondary, yeah.

114
00:08:19,146 --> 00:08:20,487
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, yeah, yeah, yeah.

115
00:08:20,527 --> 00:08:22,788
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you can find the right group, the group of arahants.

116
00:08:23,748 --> 00:08:29,490
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you seek the shelter of the... if you seek the safety, basically, and...

117
00:08:30,721 --> 00:08:35,825
[SPEAKER_00]: Follow what they say, just so you'll be safe, not because you understood it, you won't be safe.

118
00:08:36,426 --> 00:08:39,668
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can live with the Buddha himself, and you'll still be able to do jhāna.

119
00:08:41,029 --> 00:08:55,240
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're not allowing it, basically, to penetrate on the levels it should, which is on that individual basis of recognition of fundamental not-safety, non-safety, fundamental exposure of existence, fundamental impermanence, fear, and all of that.

120
00:08:58,063 --> 00:08:59,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, that's always why people go with that.

121
00:09:00,468 --> 00:09:28,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you know, often we... you saw that, like, you know, living in the various monasteries and stuff, people would... would sometimes follow or come, having been following certain teachings that they know themselves were not adequate, because they've been doing for 20 years and still haven't overcome things that even... they made them start practicing, but they couldn't give it up, because there is nothing else left, which means they would have to then accept their individual responsibility for that exposure and not knowing.

122
00:09:29,490 --> 00:09:36,395
[SPEAKER_00]: So, although they know the teachings they've been following were not adequate, they're still too afraid to admit that to themselves.

123
00:09:37,436 --> 00:09:41,960
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's all like, oh, it didn't work for me, but I mean, have you understood it or no?

124
00:09:42,320 --> 00:09:44,081
[SPEAKER_00]: Have you... you made the effort to understand.

125
00:09:44,101 --> 00:09:49,305
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what did you... to what extent did you... you must have some understanding if you recognize that it doesn't work for you.

126
00:09:50,366 --> 00:09:51,407
[SPEAKER_00]: What is that understanding?

127
00:09:52,287 --> 00:09:54,609
[SPEAKER_00]: Why it doesn't work for you?

128
00:09:54,649 --> 00:09:55,790
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it because it's inadequate?

129
00:09:55,810 --> 00:09:58,012
[SPEAKER_00]: So, or did you have a sense of, oh, this is inadequate?

130
00:09:58,931 --> 00:10:15,179
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're still afraid to take responsibility for that own recognition, and instead, I'll just pretend and ignore the inadequacy, so I can still partake in the safety of numbers, safety of tradition, safety of an established norm, and so on.

131
00:10:17,260 --> 00:10:18,940
[SPEAKER_00]: Safety of the meditation method.

132
00:10:21,021 --> 00:10:21,822
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, comfort zone.

133
00:10:21,942 --> 00:10:22,802
[SPEAKER_01]: I know what I'm doing.

134
00:10:23,002 --> 00:10:23,863
[SPEAKER_01]: I know how to do this.

135
00:10:25,154 --> 00:10:28,916
[SPEAKER_00]: I kind of, at the back of my mind, know that it hasn't resulted in things I was hoping it would.

136
00:10:28,957 --> 00:10:31,118
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't result in things that the sutta says should result.

137
00:10:31,638 --> 00:10:39,983
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yes, it's less uncomfortable than if I go against it and do away with it.

138
00:10:42,245 --> 00:10:45,807
[SPEAKER_00]: I feel less exposed if you pretend there is no problem.

139
00:10:49,549 --> 00:10:52,151
[SPEAKER_01]: So, unswayed by the emotions of the plants.

140
00:10:53,876 --> 00:10:59,640
[SPEAKER_01]: And let him never do the slightest thing that other wise men might hold blameable.

141
00:10:59,760 --> 00:11:00,001
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

142
00:11:00,661 --> 00:11:01,382
[SPEAKER_00]: The wise men.

143
00:11:01,822 --> 00:11:03,844
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the other men with right view would hold blameable.

144
00:11:04,004 --> 00:11:04,864
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is blameable?

145
00:11:05,445 --> 00:11:07,066
[SPEAKER_00]: Wrong view is the most blameworthy thing.

146
00:11:08,127 --> 00:11:12,610
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let him not entertain wrong view in any extent, in regard to anything, even for a second.

147
00:11:13,191 --> 00:11:14,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the most blameworthy thing.

148
00:11:15,232 --> 00:11:15,733
[SPEAKER_01]: But other...

149
00:11:16,173 --> 00:11:19,976
[SPEAKER_00]: Killing, stealing, cheating, lying, all wise men agree that is

150
00:11:21,290 --> 00:11:21,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Unwholesome.

151
00:11:22,070 --> 00:11:23,691
[SPEAKER_00]: And you know for yourself it's unwholesome.

152
00:11:26,251 --> 00:11:32,473
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you might not necessarily at that stage... you might not necessarily see sensuality as directly unwholesome.

153
00:11:34,533 --> 00:11:42,295
[SPEAKER_00]: But by the extension of the principles you have understood, by the extension of that wisdom, you can safely assume that it's most likely unwholesome.

154
00:11:43,296 --> 00:11:44,376
[SPEAKER_00]: And then act accordingly.

155
00:11:45,576 --> 00:11:46,376
[SPEAKER_00]: And restrain yourself.

156
00:11:46,596 --> 00:11:48,497
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you will come to realize that it wasn't wholesome.

157
00:11:54,370 --> 00:12:03,076
[SPEAKER_01]: But also, he's not swayed by the emotion of the clan, but he listens to... Oh, yeah, exactly, because he's not conceited.

158
00:12:04,077 --> 00:12:09,061
[SPEAKER_00]: He does not dismiss the clans because he is self-centered and proud.

159
00:12:10,161 --> 00:12:21,730
[SPEAKER_00]: He's not swayed by their emotions, doesn't mean he has no perspective on their emotion, which equally then means he will also have perspective on that which is factual wisdom.

160
00:12:22,872 --> 00:12:36,062
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if somebody does not belong to the clan he approves of, or he was born with, or brought up with, he would recognize when somebody says something that is correct, that is along the principle of kusala is kusala, akusala is akusala.

161
00:12:36,682 --> 00:12:38,644
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he would recognize wisdom in others.

162
00:12:44,688 --> 00:12:45,268
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's what I mean.

163
00:12:45,329 --> 00:12:52,674
[SPEAKER_00]: From from a point of view of a patujana without a right view, that sounds like the most conceited thing.

164
00:12:55,353 --> 00:12:56,633
[SPEAKER_00]: All the teachers say this.

165
00:12:57,314 --> 00:12:58,854
[SPEAKER_00]: All the Buddhist traditions say this.

166
00:12:59,254 --> 00:13:01,074
[SPEAKER_00]: And who do you think you are saying the opposite?

167
00:13:01,634 --> 00:13:07,736
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, sure, it is possible that one who is saying the opposite might be doing simply because he's so conceited.

168
00:13:08,556 --> 00:13:10,336
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he just wants to go against everybody else.

169
00:13:11,636 --> 00:13:14,657
[SPEAKER_00]: But, then you listen what else that person has to say.

170
00:13:14,837 --> 00:13:16,017
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it just a mad rant?

171
00:13:16,737 --> 00:13:19,358
[SPEAKER_00]: Or are they making sense in other regards as well?

172
00:13:19,398 --> 00:13:20,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you realize, oh, well, they are.

173
00:13:21,748 --> 00:13:28,111
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, even if the whole world says one thing, there is something to this, so maybe it's worth investigating.

174
00:13:28,591 --> 00:13:34,474
[SPEAKER_00]: It's certainly not worth dismissing it, just because majority does not agree with it.

175
00:13:35,774 --> 00:13:40,196
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, fundamentally, there is only one Buddha in the world, when it arises.

176
00:13:40,936 --> 00:13:41,877
[SPEAKER_00]: There are no two Buddhas.

177
00:13:41,957 --> 00:13:43,758
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impossible to be at the same time.

178
00:13:44,998 --> 00:13:47,179
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, the Buddha is as alone as he gets.

179
00:13:51,570 --> 00:13:59,536
[SPEAKER_00]: He's the only, the first soul being that's against the grain of the entire universe, not just this world.

180
00:14:03,719 --> 00:14:08,223
[SPEAKER_00]: And, yeah, when that man met him, straight after his enlightenment, he said, well, you look so calm.

181
00:14:08,283 --> 00:14:09,764
[SPEAKER_00]: Who's teaching did you follow?

182
00:14:09,904 --> 00:14:14,928
[SPEAKER_00]: Because this man assumed you must follow someone, some tradition, some observant, some tradition.

183
00:14:15,568 --> 00:14:18,210
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha factually said, I follow no one's teaching.

184
00:14:18,370 --> 00:14:19,171
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm my own guide.

185
00:14:19,271 --> 00:14:20,712
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm the supreme leader of men.

186
00:14:22,093 --> 00:14:23,233
[SPEAKER_00]: Pure facts.

187
00:14:24,614 --> 00:14:25,695
[SPEAKER_00]: But how did this man take it?

188
00:14:26,315 --> 00:14:27,315
[SPEAKER_00]: All right, so be it.

189
00:14:27,555 --> 00:14:28,116
[SPEAKER_00]: Good for you.

190
00:14:29,616 --> 00:14:30,116
[SPEAKER_00]: And walked off.

191
00:14:31,877 --> 00:14:36,039
[SPEAKER_00]: He did not believe him and he thought, look at this conceited fool.

192
00:14:36,059 --> 00:14:41,701
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

193
00:14:41,761 --> 00:14:43,182
[SPEAKER_00]: That should not be your criteria.

194
00:14:44,105 --> 00:14:55,391
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, he might have been factually, say, a person who declares that, might be factually conceited, or just sort of being argumentative, or just going against whatever, like steering things up.

195
00:14:56,751 --> 00:15:00,193
[SPEAKER_00]: But then you listen what else they have to say, and then you'll see.

196
00:15:00,533 --> 00:15:02,154
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, wait, there is something more to this.

197
00:15:03,535 --> 00:15:04,856
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that they're just negating.

198
00:15:05,276 --> 00:15:07,077
[SPEAKER_00]: They're providing reasons for the negating.

199
00:15:07,617 --> 00:15:09,598
[SPEAKER_00]: They're providing rational explanations.

200
00:15:10,118 --> 00:15:13,400
[SPEAKER_00]: They tie in with other things that, you know, could be interpreted that way.

201
00:15:16,842 --> 00:15:20,585
[SPEAKER_00]: And if that's the case, then you should investigate it further, and then use that as a criteria.

202
00:15:20,605 --> 00:15:27,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because, oh, a hundred people say one thing, and one person says the other.

203
00:15:27,670 --> 00:15:28,350
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, he's wrong.

204
00:15:30,011 --> 00:15:41,720
[SPEAKER_00]: And sometimes people even know that person in minority is not wrong, but they still try to argue a point that you should still go with the majority, and not disturb the established order of majority.

205
00:15:43,049 --> 00:15:52,335
[SPEAKER_00]: That is true only when the... if... when non-disturbing of the established order of the clans, of the tradition, would not go against the message you have.

206
00:15:53,996 --> 00:16:02,421
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you can go with the order, without compromising the message that you have, which is against what the order's message is, then it's fine.

207
00:16:02,461 --> 00:16:03,361
[SPEAKER_00]: You can go with the order.

208
00:16:04,382 --> 00:16:12,627
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you can... if going with the order means compromising the message that you have, that is correct, that the whole order disagrees with, then you shouldn't go with the order.

209
00:16:13,370 --> 00:16:17,311
[SPEAKER_00]: You should stick with the message, because, well, that's the only thing that matters.

210
00:16:17,891 --> 00:16:18,751
[SPEAKER_00]: Right view or wrong view.

211
00:16:18,831 --> 00:16:21,152
[SPEAKER_00]: The wrong view, as the Buddha said, is the most blameworthy thing.

212
00:16:21,532 --> 00:16:24,132
[SPEAKER_00]: More blameworthy than killing, stealing, cheating and everything else.

213
00:16:25,152 --> 00:16:30,253
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the utmost, single, blameworthy thing in the entire universe, in the entire saṃsara.

214
00:16:31,494 --> 00:16:32,714
[SPEAKER_00]: Thinking to know that which you don't.

215
00:16:34,734 --> 00:16:38,095
[SPEAKER_00]: Holding on to a view without questioning it, without understanding it.

216
00:16:46,295 --> 00:16:49,116
[SPEAKER_01]: It goes to... becomes a bit different, this.

217
00:16:49,436 --> 00:17:00,139
[SPEAKER_01]: So, let's talk about what this person should be thinking, in terms of that metta, peaceful mind.

218
00:17:01,279 --> 00:17:05,661
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, let him think in safety and in happiness.

219
00:17:05,881 --> 00:17:08,742
[SPEAKER_01]: May creatures all be of a happy mind.

220
00:17:10,543 --> 00:17:18,248
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever breathing beings there may be, no matter what they are, eh... may creatures all be of a happy mind.

221
00:17:19,769 --> 00:17:26,813
[SPEAKER_01]: Let no one work for another's undoing, or even slight him at all anywhere.

222
00:17:31,394 --> 00:17:52,751
[SPEAKER_00]: So, on the level of your thoughts, do not entertain even slightest, like, negative animosity, ill will, and so on.

223
00:17:52,851 --> 00:17:53,512
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter.

224
00:17:54,172 --> 00:17:55,493
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter what happens to you.

225
00:17:56,454 --> 00:17:57,475
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter on account of

226
00:17:58,994 --> 00:18:07,136
[SPEAKER_00]: What things happen to you, doesn't matter from whom, from what type of being, don't have a limited mind towards them.

227
00:18:08,457 --> 00:18:15,639
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you... because most people would take this and say, okay, this is the mantra that I must now say a million times.

228
00:18:15,699 --> 00:18:16,659
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll say it a million times.

229
00:18:17,479 --> 00:18:20,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Insectian, blossom-like, may creatures not be.

230
00:18:20,320 --> 00:18:21,961
[SPEAKER_01]: And on and on and on and on and on and on.

231
00:18:23,501 --> 00:18:24,921
[SPEAKER_01]: And you do that, do that, do that, do that.

232
00:18:25,321 --> 00:18:26,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you ask yourself,

233
00:18:27,381 --> 00:18:34,190
[SPEAKER_00]: Does repeating a mantra cultivate, maintain the understanding of the point of that mantra?

234
00:18:35,131 --> 00:18:36,373
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is it actually quite the opposite?

235
00:18:37,294 --> 00:18:40,859
[SPEAKER_00]: Focusing on repeating the mantra means ignoring the fact that it needs to be understood.

236
00:18:44,027 --> 00:18:46,327
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you... so, if you had to...

237
00:18:47,128 --> 00:18:55,989
[SPEAKER_01]: I remember doing a similar thing a while... a longer... repeating certain... not... certain phrases.

238
00:18:56,049 --> 00:18:57,509
[SPEAKER_01]: May my mother be well.

239
00:18:57,570 --> 00:18:58,790
[SPEAKER_01]: May my sister this.

240
00:18:58,970 --> 00:19:00,770
[SPEAKER_01]: May I be this, that, that.

241
00:19:01,750 --> 00:19:05,231
[SPEAKER_01]: And you generate... it's quite a pleasant thought.

242
00:19:06,071 --> 00:19:07,151
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have pleasant feelings.

243
00:19:08,271 --> 00:19:09,032
[SPEAKER_01]: And you feel good.

244
00:19:09,152 --> 00:19:11,012
[SPEAKER_01]: You just feel... Yeah.

245
00:19:12,175 --> 00:19:13,276
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not what it says there.

246
00:19:13,736 --> 00:19:20,440
[SPEAKER_00]: It says you should cultivate that mind, the state of mind that has no animosity towards anything, breathing or any kind.

247
00:19:21,701 --> 00:19:24,482
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't cultivate state of mind by repeating the random words.

248
00:19:25,703 --> 00:19:27,584
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if that would be the case, the Buddha would have said so.

249
00:19:27,784 --> 00:19:30,005
[SPEAKER_00]: Just chant this and you'll be purified.

250
00:19:30,085 --> 00:19:37,009
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, whenever he talked about it, he talked about it in a dismissive manner of the other Brahmanic traditions they were doing.

251
00:19:37,810 --> 00:19:41,252
[SPEAKER_00]: And he would criticize them for chanting Vedas without understanding what they're chanting.

252
00:19:42,212 --> 00:19:48,355
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they're just parroting, basically, the verses, because they developed the belief that the verses in themselves carry magic of purification.

253
00:19:49,135 --> 00:19:56,059
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, he said that even the original Brahmins that sort of compiled those Vedas and and and the wisdom in them, they actually understood them.

254
00:19:57,419 --> 00:19:58,540
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why they were worthy men.

255
00:20:00,441 --> 00:20:03,082
[SPEAKER_00]: Brahmins of the old day, they understood the verses.

256
00:20:03,142 --> 00:20:04,242
[SPEAKER_00]: They understood the meaning.

257
00:20:04,282 --> 00:20:05,523
[SPEAKER_00]: They were not just chanting them.

258
00:20:06,383 --> 00:20:11,666
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you chant for the sake of remembering it, for the sake of then reflecting on it, to understand it, of course,

259
00:20:12,837 --> 00:20:19,262
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you chant because you believe that chanting purifies you and maintains your mind of non-ill will, that's an utterly, completely wrong view.

260
00:20:20,323 --> 00:20:25,887
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, these people say this, I will say that as well.

261
00:20:25,907 --> 00:20:27,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

262
00:20:28,549 --> 00:20:29,970
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how I will partake in that.

263
00:20:30,030 --> 00:20:30,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I'm safe.

264
00:20:31,951 --> 00:20:32,352
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm in it.

265
00:20:33,413 --> 00:20:38,837
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I will just go by the hearsay, tradition and repeating words of others.

266
00:20:39,840 --> 00:20:43,401
[SPEAKER_00]: By mere repeating words of others, I will purify myself.

267
00:20:43,501 --> 00:20:48,242
[SPEAKER_00]: That's basically practically what that view is, for those who believe that just chanting will purify you.

268
00:20:51,303 --> 00:20:52,363
[SPEAKER_01]: But let him think.

269
00:20:52,443 --> 00:20:53,623
[SPEAKER_01]: Let him think about this.

270
00:20:54,303 --> 00:20:55,284
[SPEAKER_00]: Let him reflect upon it.

271
00:20:55,304 --> 00:20:56,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Let him reflect.

272
00:20:56,984 --> 00:21:05,286
[SPEAKER_00]: Let him develop the state of mind that is limitless, that includes all these things you can have animosity towards.

273
00:21:06,546 --> 00:21:09,007
[SPEAKER_00]: So you develop a state of mind free from animosity.

274
00:21:09,979 --> 00:21:13,401
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all the things within that, that you could hate, you cannot hate.

275
00:21:14,642 --> 00:21:18,024
[SPEAKER_00]: Because hate is rooted in the state of mind, not in things that you hate.

276
00:21:20,986 --> 00:21:23,908
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can also ask... yeah, you think about it.

277
00:21:24,368 --> 00:21:27,790
[SPEAKER_01]: In safety and in happiness, may creatures all be... Yeah.

278
00:21:27,830 --> 00:21:31,793
[SPEAKER_00]: Think about those verses, about guidelines for your contemplation.

279
00:21:31,833 --> 00:21:32,273
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

280
00:21:32,293 --> 00:21:32,513
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

281
00:21:32,633 --> 00:21:33,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do it?

282
00:21:33,153 --> 00:21:33,394
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

283
00:21:33,414 --> 00:21:35,355
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the Buddha said nothing that breaches... Why is that?

284
00:21:35,555 --> 00:21:35,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

285
00:21:36,846 --> 00:21:43,111
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, if you can't figure out why, take the Buddha's word and trust, then figure out how to achieve that.

286
00:21:43,451 --> 00:21:52,718
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how will I have one state of mind that will not hate this wide array of diverse beings and objects?

287
00:21:54,799 --> 00:21:59,683
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that achievable by chasing down the diversity of the objects?

288
00:22:00,830 --> 00:22:06,712
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is it achievable by maintaining the uniformity of one non-hateful mind?

289
00:22:07,793 --> 00:22:08,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is more likely?

290
00:22:10,174 --> 00:22:15,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it about cultivating the pleasure of a particular thought?

291
00:22:17,797 --> 00:22:21,739
[SPEAKER_01]: And the avoidance of the displeasure of... Another particular thought.

292
00:22:21,759 --> 00:22:22,279
[SPEAKER_00]: Another thought.

293
00:22:23,019 --> 00:22:29,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is it cultivating a thought... That is above either pleasure...

294
00:22:30,465 --> 00:22:31,206
[SPEAKER_00]: Or displeasure.

295
00:22:31,266 --> 00:22:32,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

296
00:22:32,427 --> 00:22:33,348
[SPEAKER_01]: The answer is obvious.

297
00:22:33,488 --> 00:22:33,988
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

298
00:22:38,692 --> 00:22:39,293
[SPEAKER_01]: It continues.

299
00:22:39,953 --> 00:22:52,405
[SPEAKER_01]: And just as a mother with her life protects her only child, so let him then, for every living thing, protect his boundless mind.

300
00:22:52,425 --> 00:22:53,686
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

301
00:22:53,706 --> 00:22:55,207
[SPEAKER_01]: We spoke about it before.

302
00:22:55,347 --> 00:22:56,048
[SPEAKER_00]: It's quite obvious.

303
00:22:57,307 --> 00:23:05,371
[SPEAKER_00]: You have contemporary translations that skew that, and they basically imply that, as the mother protects the child, you protect all these living beings.

304
00:23:05,631 --> 00:23:06,071
[SPEAKER_01]: Does it?

305
00:23:06,551 --> 00:23:09,273
[SPEAKER_01]: So, let him protect every living being?

306
00:23:09,413 --> 00:23:10,393
[SPEAKER_00]: Like his own child.

307
00:23:11,193 --> 00:23:16,016
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, let him protect his one mind, like the mother protects one child, one son, literally.

308
00:23:17,116 --> 00:23:21,238
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your only son, that's your mind.

309
00:23:21,638 --> 00:23:22,919
[SPEAKER_00]: Your mind is your only child.

310
00:23:23,804 --> 00:23:30,109
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you will not forget about cultivation, protection and maintenance of that child.

311
00:23:30,149 --> 00:23:32,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you do, it could die.

312
00:23:33,873 --> 00:23:35,294
[SPEAKER_00]: And it could result in your own death.

313
00:23:36,435 --> 00:23:43,000
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you protect your mind as your own child, you will automatically protect all living beings.

314
00:23:43,040 --> 00:23:51,828
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when there is action of non-protection of all living beings, it's not rooted in those beings or non those beings, it's rooted in your mind.

315
00:23:53,120 --> 00:24:01,569
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you protect your mind from ill-will, no acts of ill-will can be exercised towards anything in the world.

316
00:24:02,110 --> 00:24:05,073
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by proxy, then, you protect the entire world.

317
00:24:05,593 --> 00:24:11,179
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how the Buddha explained arahant, by practicing for his own benefit, practicing for the benefit of the whole universe.

318
00:24:14,544 --> 00:24:17,028
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but we want to... yeah, if you want to see... Well, no.

319
00:24:17,048 --> 00:24:20,653
[SPEAKER_00]: Most people, that internal transparent dimension is not transparent.

320
00:24:21,374 --> 00:24:22,616
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... they don't see it.

321
00:24:23,488 --> 00:24:30,731
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they measure goodness or non-goodness by the level of engagement with others and with the world.

322
00:24:31,271 --> 00:24:37,554
[SPEAKER_00]: So, from that point of view, of an ignorant puthujjana's view, Arahant is the most selfish person in the world.

323
00:24:37,814 --> 00:24:39,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Swayed by the emotions.

324
00:24:39,755 --> 00:24:39,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

325
00:24:40,075 --> 00:24:41,356
[SPEAKER_00]: Swayed by the emotions of others.

326
00:24:41,396 --> 00:24:43,117
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

327
00:24:43,217 --> 00:24:47,458
[SPEAKER_00]: Using the measure of emotions for whether things are good or bad.

328
00:24:48,519 --> 00:24:50,380
[SPEAKER_00]: This makes us feel good, thus it's good.

329
00:24:50,660 --> 00:24:51,000
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

330
00:24:52,559 --> 00:24:57,864
[SPEAKER_00]: And, you see, ironically then, many people approach the dhamma practice rooted in that.

331
00:24:58,945 --> 00:25:04,790
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I'll practice freedom from my dependence on feelings by following what makes me feel good.

332
00:25:04,810 --> 00:25:06,011
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a contradiction in terms.

333
00:25:11,196 --> 00:25:14,018
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what we said that in the previous... one of the previous talks.

334
00:25:15,360 --> 00:25:17,642
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't remember what order we shared in, but...

335
00:25:19,131 --> 00:25:25,777
[SPEAKER_00]: If you just... you don't need to practice kindness and non-ill will towards the world, or towards anyone in particular.

336
00:25:26,217 --> 00:25:29,200
[SPEAKER_00]: Just practice it towards whatever present feeling is enduring in your experience.

337
00:25:29,780 --> 00:25:31,722
[SPEAKER_00]: And you've covered... And you've covered the entire world.

338
00:25:33,603 --> 00:25:40,809
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything to breathe, you cannot hate, because you stop resisting the discomfort of a feeling that's presently enduring.

339
00:25:40,849 --> 00:25:42,311
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you truly want to help,

340
00:25:43,320 --> 00:25:44,281
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the only way you can help.

341
00:25:44,701 --> 00:25:59,214
[SPEAKER_00]: You can have a desire to help, but you can choose to ignore understanding what is truly helpful on account of that desire, or you can choose to not ignore and make the effort to understand what is truly helpful.

342
00:26:00,115 --> 00:26:02,357
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is truly helpful is the mind of non-ill will.

343
00:26:08,639 --> 00:26:12,481
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you can do that, then you can potentially teach others to do that.

344
00:26:12,541 --> 00:26:13,641
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course.

345
00:26:13,821 --> 00:26:14,001
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

346
00:26:16,122 --> 00:26:16,963
[SPEAKER_01]: Which protects them.

347
00:26:17,603 --> 00:26:17,783
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

348
00:26:17,963 --> 00:26:24,906
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can build people houses, food, give them food... That's all mundane, mundane pertaining to the world.

349
00:26:24,986 --> 00:26:33,450
[SPEAKER_00]: In that sense, it's not a... You can do that, if that's your goal, but you can't expect to do that and practice dhamma at the same time, because those goals are mutually exclusive.

350
00:26:34,663 --> 00:26:41,805
[SPEAKER_00]: If you can help others and not compromise the lifestyle that's dedicated to practicing the dhamma that you understood, that's fine, of course.

351
00:26:42,305 --> 00:26:43,725
[SPEAKER_00]: Somebody's drowning, you pull them out.

352
00:26:43,865 --> 00:26:47,786
[SPEAKER_00]: You're like, oh, well, I will teach them dhamma in these three seconds before they drown.

353
00:26:49,207 --> 00:27:01,950
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you dedicate your life to saving people from drowning, in those particular instances, and on account of that neglect your own mind, your own child of non-ill will, well, that doesn't help anyone.

354
00:27:03,477 --> 00:27:06,499
[SPEAKER_00]: The help is very limited, very fleeting.

355
00:27:07,319 --> 00:27:07,519
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

356
00:27:08,100 --> 00:27:08,820
[SPEAKER_00]: Not substantial.

357
00:27:12,102 --> 00:27:25,510
[SPEAKER_01]: So, above, below, and all around, untroubled, with no enemy, and while he stands, walks, sits, or lies down, free from drowsiness,

358
00:27:26,649 --> 00:27:28,750
[SPEAKER_01]: Let him resolve upon this mindfulness.

359
00:27:29,070 --> 00:27:29,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

360
00:27:30,250 --> 00:27:31,671
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the peaceful life.

361
00:27:31,871 --> 00:27:32,111
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

362
00:27:32,571 --> 00:27:37,333
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot resolve upon things that are not clear.

363
00:27:40,755 --> 00:27:46,217
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, there's instances in the suttas of a person who becomes an arahant by resolving upon wisdom.

364
00:27:49,458 --> 00:27:53,780
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he resolves upon that which he gained insight with wisdom.

365
00:27:53,880 --> 00:27:54,100
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

366
00:27:55,947 --> 00:27:58,949
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in order to resolve upon something, you need to know what that is.

367
00:28:00,610 --> 00:28:01,731
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to be clear about it.

368
00:28:01,751 --> 00:28:02,952
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to be familiar with it.

369
00:28:03,132 --> 00:28:09,977
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to have made the effort to remove any opaqueness, any ambiguity, in regard to the meaning and nature of it.

370
00:28:10,658 --> 00:28:14,440
[SPEAKER_00]: Once it's sufficiently clear, that's when that resolve can occur.

371
00:28:14,640 --> 00:28:16,442
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when you can maintain that resolve.

372
00:28:16,522 --> 00:28:18,083
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it can result in arahantship.

373
00:28:20,089 --> 00:28:28,853
[SPEAKER_00]: It says free from drowsiness, but free from... Free from sloth, free from lack of clarity, free from laziness in regard to maintaining the clarity.

374
00:28:33,795 --> 00:28:43,560
[SPEAKER_01]: Not somehow energetic, like magical energy, or free from... it's not tired.

375
00:28:45,078 --> 00:28:49,680
[SPEAKER_01]: He's got this abundant energy flowing through his body of love.

376
00:28:49,800 --> 00:28:50,101
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

377
00:28:50,141 --> 00:28:50,901
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's not.

378
00:28:50,981 --> 00:28:52,062
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

379
00:28:52,602 --> 00:28:58,565
[SPEAKER_00]: It refers to the clarity of the meaning, which is why you can resolve upon it with mindfulness.

380
00:28:58,585 --> 00:28:59,846
[SPEAKER_01]: Not this energy of love.

381
00:28:59,886 --> 00:29:12,192
[SPEAKER_00]: You can be tired, but tiredness does not induce losing the sight of the right context, does not induce forgetting about your only child.

382
00:29:13,053 --> 00:29:13,593
[SPEAKER_00]: You're tired.

383
00:29:14,646 --> 00:29:16,247
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're still clear about what's important.

384
00:29:17,387 --> 00:29:19,548
[SPEAKER_00]: You're still clear about what you must protect.

385
00:29:20,989 --> 00:29:21,749
[SPEAKER_00]: You still protect it.

386
00:29:22,249 --> 00:29:24,270
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when you're not tired, you protect it even more.

387
00:29:25,031 --> 00:29:25,731
[SPEAKER_00]: You cultivate it.

388
00:29:30,053 --> 00:29:33,274
[SPEAKER_01]: The last verse is...

389
00:29:33,654 --> 00:29:34,295
[SPEAKER_01]: When he has...

390
00:29:36,496 --> 00:29:38,376
[SPEAKER_01]: When he's not holding to any views...

391
00:29:40,755 --> 00:29:47,299
[SPEAKER_01]: He's virtuous and has perfected seeing and purges greed from... for sensual desires.

392
00:29:48,439 --> 00:29:48,819
[SPEAKER_01]: He surely...

393
00:29:48,839 --> 00:29:50,220
[SPEAKER_00]: But not holding to wrong views.

394
00:29:50,240 --> 00:29:51,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, not holding to wrong views.

395
00:29:51,741 --> 00:29:54,703
[SPEAKER_01]: Virtuous, perfecting knowledge and seeing.

396
00:29:56,304 --> 00:29:57,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Free from sensual desires.

397
00:29:57,925 --> 00:29:59,946
[SPEAKER_01]: He no longer gets born again.

398
00:29:59,966 --> 00:30:02,327
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when arahant becomes... he goes beyond the views.

399
00:30:02,707 --> 00:30:03,368
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it means.

400
00:30:03,688 --> 00:30:03,908
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

401
00:30:04,605 --> 00:30:12,730
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need the right view in order to overcome the wrong views, but for an arahant, basically, he overcomes even the right view.

402
00:30:16,233 --> 00:30:28,721
[SPEAKER_00]: That's that sutra, it has the Noble Eightfold Path, and for an arahant, it's like a tenfold path, whereby he overcomes even that dependence on the view, the right view, the right criteria, he even extinguishes that.

403
00:30:29,961 --> 00:30:33,564
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he resolves upon clarity... With the right view?

404
00:30:33,724 --> 00:30:33,964
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

405
00:30:35,028 --> 00:30:38,633
[SPEAKER_00]: You maintain it, you cultivate it until it ripens, and then even that falls off.

406
00:30:39,354 --> 00:30:44,701
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, once you use the raft that you built, you cross the shore, and then you leave the raft.

407
00:30:45,161 --> 00:30:46,543
[SPEAKER_00]: And when do you leave the raft?

408
00:30:46,603 --> 00:30:48,886
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you know when you're safe on the other side of the shore.

409
00:30:49,767 --> 00:30:51,209
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like halfway through pedaling.

410
00:30:52,162 --> 00:30:53,504
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, do I abandon raft now?

411
00:30:53,784 --> 00:30:57,068
[SPEAKER_00]: Or... You cannot doubt it, because it's not up to you anymore.

412
00:30:57,088 --> 00:30:57,529
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

413
00:30:57,889 --> 00:31:00,592
[SPEAKER_01]: They say that you abandon this raft when you get to the other shore.

414
00:31:00,813 --> 00:31:01,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, I'll abandon it now.

415
00:31:02,555 --> 00:31:03,796
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right, right.

416
00:31:03,836 --> 00:31:04,197
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

417
00:31:04,257 --> 00:31:07,000
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll preemptively abandon it, because you shouldn't hold on to anything.

418
00:31:07,040 --> 00:31:07,281
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

419
00:31:08,167 --> 00:31:09,688
[SPEAKER_00]: No, then that's often misunderstood.

420
00:31:09,728 --> 00:31:20,136
[SPEAKER_00]: I know in the same, like the contemporary chanting of that, like, not hold to any views, or they even now, I think, since in some translations, don't hold to fixed views.

421
00:31:20,156 --> 00:31:21,617
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, well, that's a fixed view.

422
00:31:22,397 --> 00:31:25,079
[SPEAKER_00]: I shall not hold to fixed views is your fixed view.

423
00:31:26,200 --> 00:31:35,867
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is no way out of that, except finding the right type of fixed view, which is the fixed view of Sotāpanna, that he developed through understanding the Buddha's teaching.

424
00:31:36,885 --> 00:31:44,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, using that fixed view to overcome all the kind of underlying tendencies and kilesas and so on, then that fixed view will wither away as well.

425
00:31:46,451 --> 00:31:47,592
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when you're an arahant.

426
00:31:49,253 --> 00:31:59,659
[SPEAKER_00]: But people think, oh, if I abstain from declaring my view, I somehow skate between the views and I will be free from the views by not holding on to views, which is the view that I hold.

427
00:32:02,521 --> 00:32:04,482
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that last verse refers to the arahantship.

428
00:32:07,092 --> 00:32:17,157
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the only one truly who can say, I don't hold to any views anymore, is the one whose right view has withered away, extinguished itself through the right effort.

429
00:32:20,179 --> 00:32:21,199
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he went beyond the views.

430
00:32:24,781 --> 00:32:31,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's the full development of...

431
00:32:32,542 --> 00:32:33,563
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's not a choice.

432
00:32:33,843 --> 00:32:38,545
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not so that when he makes a choice, I will not hold to my right view and he becomes an arahant.

433
00:32:39,286 --> 00:32:40,907
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a result of the right practice.

434
00:32:41,787 --> 00:32:49,632
[SPEAKER_00]: That the Buddha outlines there and then says, if you practice like that, you will not anymore be attached to any view, even the right view.

435
00:32:50,172 --> 00:32:51,253
[SPEAKER_00]: You will be an arahant then.

436
00:32:52,954 --> 00:32:55,055
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's the right view that brought him there.

437
00:32:55,095 --> 00:32:57,096
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the raft that took him to the other shore.

